(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-cms-exam-7-master-exam-form-a"]={"title":"CMS I Exam 7 Master Exam — Form A","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A woman answers yes to loud snoring and to being observed to stop breathing during sleep; her body mass index is 38 kg/m2 and her shirt collar is 16 inches. She is not tired during the day, is not treated for high blood pressure and is not older than 50. What is her STOP-Bang (snoring, tiredness, observed apnea, pressure, body mass index, age, neck, gender) total?","choices":["3","4","5","6"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Snoring 1 + observed apnea 1 + body mass index above 35 1 + neck 16 inches or larger 1 = 4; tiredness, pressure, age and gender (female) score 0. A total of 4 is intermediate, but yes to 2 of the first four questions plus a body mass index above 35 makes her high risk. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man with pulmonary arterial hypertension, functional class II, asks how to protect his health between visits. His resting oxygen saturation is 96%, and it falls to 87% when he walks briskly. Which recommendation should be avoided?","choices":["Oxygen during exertion and sleep","Supervised aerobic rehabilitation","Annual influenza immunization","Strict bed rest to save energy"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Strict bed rest should be avoided in pulmonary hypertension: supervised, steady aerobic rehabilitation is safe, prevents muscle deconditioning and improves fatigue. Resting to spare the heart only adds deconditioning to his disease. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 72-year-old woman has chronic respiratory acidosis from long-standing chronic obstructive pulmonary disease. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.33 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 68 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 35 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). A student predicts her kidneys will bring her pH back to 7.40 with time. Which statement about her compensation is accurate?","choices":["It moves pH toward normal without fully correcting it","It overshoots, leaving her slightly alkalemic","It works through the lungs blowing off carbon dioxide","It fully restores pH once 48 hours have passed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Compensation will not fully correct the pH disturbance; the underlying condition must be corrected to restore balance. Her kidneys have raised the bicarbonate to 35, which pulls the pH up toward normal, but it remains 7.33. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 23, 34"},{"q":"A 78-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease has a second secondary spontaneous pneumothorax, now drained by a chest tube. His team judges that he cannot tolerate general anesthesia, so surgery is not an option. Which procedure best lowers his risk of another recurrence?","choices":["Repeated catheter aspiration","Video-assisted thoracoscopic surgery","Chemical pleurodesis via chest tube","Mechanical pleurodesis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Most secondary spontaneous pneumothoraces are treated with a chest tube and may go on to thoracoscopic surgery or thoracotomy with bleb stapling and pleural abrasion. If surgery is not an option, chemical pleurodesis via the chest tube is used: a sclerosant such as talc makes the pleural layers adhere. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 39, 46, 49, 57"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man inhaled chlorine gas in an industrial accident. Six hours later he has severe dyspnea and bilateral infiltrates. Echocardiography is normal, and his pulmonary capillary wedge pressure is 8 mmHg. Which mechanism best explains his pulmonary edema?","choices":["Regurgitant flow across a heart valve","Diastolic stiffening of the ventricle","Hydrostatic backup from the left heart","Toxic injury to the capillary membrane"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Inhaled toxins cause chemical injury to the alveolar-capillary membrane, so the barrier shatters and protein-rich fluid leaks into the alveoli: noncardiogenic, permeability edema. His normal echocardiogram and wedge pressure of 8 mmHg exclude a pressure problem. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 33, 34"},{"q":"A 46-year-old man with severe pancreatitis develops profound dyspnea on hospital day 2. His chest film shows new bilateral opacities not explained by effusion, atelectasis or nodules, and an echocardiogram shows normal cardiac function. His arterial oxygen pressure is 62 mm Hg on a fraction of inspired oxygen of 0.40. How is his oxygenation impairment classified?","choices":["Oxygenation criterion not met","Mild acute respiratory distress syndrome","Moderate acute respiratory distress syndrome","Severe acute respiratory distress syndrome"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Divide arterial oxygen pressure by fraction of inspired oxygen: 62 / 0.40 = 155 mm Hg. That is under the 300 threshold and falls between 100 and 200, the moderate class. Severe pancreatitis is a systemic trigger, and his new opacities and normal heart complete the criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 73"},{"q":"A 63-year-old man on peritoneal dialysis develops a right pleural effusion. He has no heart failure, and his serum albumin is normal at 4.0 g/dL (normal 3.5-5.0 g/dL). He asks how the dialysis fluid in his abdomen could reach his chest. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["It lowers oncotic pressure","It raises capillary pressure","It blocks lymph drainage","It crosses the diaphragm"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. In cirrhosis and peritoneal dialysis, increased peritoneal fluid leaks, or extravasates, across the diaphragm through microperforations into the pleural space. Peritoneal dialysis is listed among the conditions associated with transudates. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 81, 82, 111"},{"q":"A 23-year-old man asks why trivial details of his day seem to fade after a night's sleep while what mattered stays. Which function of sleep explains this?","choices":["Clearing metabolic waste","Facilitating learning or memory","Targeted erasure of synapses","Neural maturation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. One function of sleep is targeted erasure of synapses to forget unimportant information that might otherwise clutter the synaptic network. Losing trivial details while keeping what matters fits that function, which works alongside sleep's facilitation of learning or memory. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 17"},{"q":"A 25-year-old woman has sudden left-sided pleuritic chest pain and dyspnea at rest. Her upright chest radiograph is reviewed for a pneumothorax. Which radiographic finding establishes the diagnosis?","choices":["Straight air-fluid line","Blunted costophrenic angle","Markings beyond the pleural edge","Visceral pleural line"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The diagnosis is established by demonstrating a visceral pleural line, the interface of lung and pleural air. It is a thin, sharp line convex outward toward the chest wall, with no bronchovascular markings visible beyond it. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 71, 90"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man who is fit for surgery has an image-guided biopsy of a 22-mm spiculated (spiky) upper-lobe nodule that shows no malignancy. The nodule has enlarged on serial scans, and his team is concerned the biopsy missed the tumor. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Annual screening scans","Chemotherapy now","Repeat biopsy or excision","Reassure and discharge"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. When an image-guided biopsy does not confirm malignancy, the pathway is to consider a repeat biopsy or to proceed to excision if there is concern about a false-negative biopsy. His spiky border, upper-lobe location and growth over time are all concerning features, so a negative result does not end the workup. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 15, 16, 35"},{"q":"A 67-year-old man with lung adenocarcinoma has metastases in the liver, an adrenal gland and several bones, placing him at stage 4B. Which treatment is standard for his cancer?","choices":["Lobectomy","Chemotherapy","Observation","Pneumonectomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. For non-small cell lung cancer, stage 4 is treated with chemotherapy, with surgical resection considered only for a solitary metastatic lesion with a resectable primary tumor. Chemotherapy and radiation are used as adjuncts to surgery or when the patient is stage 4. He has metastases at several sites, so resection is not the plan. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 35"},{"q":"Which nodule appearance is concerning for malignancy?","choices":["Well-defined border","Popcorn calcification","Internal fat","Spiculated border"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. A spiculated appearance, with spikes, deviations or lobulations in the border, is one of the concerning nodule features; the invasive adenocarcinoma example is a suspicious solid spiculated nodule. Other concerning features include size over 6 mm, upper lobe or hilar location and growth over time. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 16, 21, 32"},{"q":"A 72-year-old man with severe chronic obstructive pulmonary disease develops a spontaneous pneumothorax. His daughter, who had a pneumothorax at age 24 with no lung disease, asks how his episode is likely to differ from hers. Which statement is accurate?","choices":["Milder; good reserve","Less urgent; home observation","Same severity and same care","More severe; less reserve"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease is the most common cause of secondary spontaneous pneumothorax. The symptoms are those of a primary episode but generally greater, because the underlying disease leaves less pulmonary reserve; the event can be life-threatening and needs more immediate intervention. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 20, 24, 53, 56, 57"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with nephrotic syndrome and a urinary infection has a serum albumin 2.5 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.36 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 37 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 20 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Using 10 as the normal anion gap and 25 mEq/L as the normal bicarbonate, what is her change in anion gap once the gap is corrected for albumin?","choices":["4","9","14","19"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (104 + 20) = 14. Albumin is 2 below 4.5, so add 2.5 x 2 = 5: corrected gap = 19. Change in gap = 19 - 10 = 9. Against a change in bicarbonate of 25 - 20 = 5, the gap rose more. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 50, 8, 57"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman is leaving the hospital after acute respiratory distress syndrome from pneumonia. She asks how long her lung function will take to recover. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["About 1 month","About 3 months","About 6 to 12 months","About 36 months"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lung function generally recovers over 6 to 12 months after acute respiratory distress syndrome, though functional deficits often remain, with residual diffusion deficits persisting. Physical sequelae after intensive care, such as muscle wasting, weakness and fatigue, also need attention. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 72, 74"},{"q":"A 49-year-old woman is evaluated for fatigue. Her arterial blood gas shows pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 40 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 24 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). A serum panel drawn at the same moment shows a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Recheck both, as the bicarbonates disagree","Use the panel bicarbonate and proceed","Average the two values and proceed","Use the gas bicarbonate and proceed"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The bicarbonate on the blood gas and on the electrolytes is compared to verify accuracy before any further step. A difference of 10 mEq/L between samples drawn together means one result is unreliable, so neither can carry the analysis yet. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 56, 57"},{"q":"Popcorn calcification in a lung nodule points to which lesion?","choices":["Hamartoma","Adenocarcinoma","Carcinoid tumor","Healed granuloma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Benign nodules tend to have well-defined borders and may contain fatty tissue or calcifications; popcorn-looking calcification means a hamartoma, a connective tissue growth. On computed tomography a hamartoma is a smoothly marginated solid nodule with internal fat and calcification. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 4, 12, 16, 21, 32, 48"},{"q":"A 28-year-old man has had mild left-sided chest wall pain for a day, reproduced by pressing on a rib cartilage. His Wells score is 0, and the clinician's gestalt for pulmonary embolism is very low (under 15%). His heart rate is 78/min and oxygen saturation 99%. He has no hemoptysis, takes no estrogen, has never had a clot, has had no recent surgery or trauma and has no leg swelling. Applying the PERC (Pulmonary Embolism Rule-out Criteria), which step fits?","choices":["Ventilation-perfusion scan","Computed tomographic angiography","Stop: no further testing","Order a D-dimer to be safe"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. He meets all eight criteria: age under 50, heart rate under 100, saturation above 94%, no hemoptysis, no estrogen, no prior venous thromboembolism, no surgery or trauma and no unilateral leg swelling. With very low gestalt and every criterion met, the workup stops. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 54, 57"},{"q":"A 44-year-old man with obstructive sleep apnea has respiratory events on polysomnogram that occur when he sleeps on his back and not when he lies on his side. He wants a measure to add to his care. Which therapy targets this pattern?","choices":["Nasal steroid spray","Positional therapy","Palatal implants","Tracheostomy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Positional therapy fits when events occur in the supine position: a sleep belt or sleep shirt keeps him lying on his side. His events are confined to back-sleeping, the exact pattern it addresses. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 12, 13"},{"q":"A 35-year-old man's 8-hour sleep recording is scored stage by stage. Which stage takes up the largest share of his total sleep time?","choices":["Stage N2","Rapid eye movement sleep","Stage N1","Stage N3"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stage N2 makes up the majority of the sleep cycle and is the longest stage by total duration. Rapid eye movement sleep fills about 25% of a young adult's sleep, and stage N2 outlasts both the brief drowsy stage N1 and the deep stage N3, so it holds the largest share of his night. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 13, 28, 9"},{"q":"A 41-year-old woman has bipolar disorder treated with lithium and type 2 diabetes treated with metformin. Routine testing shows sodium 136 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 106 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 26 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L), albumin 4.4 g/dL (normal 3.5 to 5.5 g/dL), and no monoclonal protein. Which part of her history best accounts for her anion gap?","choices":["A plasma cell dyscrasia","A low serum albumin","Metformin","Lithium"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Her gap is 136 - (26 + 106) = 4, below the normal 6 to 12. A decreased gap comes from added abnormal cations such as lithium, which are not counted in the formula. Her normal albumin and absent monoclonal protein remove the other low-gap causes. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 7, 48"},{"q":"A 65-year-old woman with a biopsy-proven 4-cm non-small cell carcinoma has a staging scan showing metastases in the right adrenal gland and in several vertebrae. Which stage is her cancer?","choices":["Stage 3B","Stage 3C","Stage 4A","Stage 4B"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Multiple extrathoracic metastases, in one or more organs, are scored M1c, and spread to multiple sites outside the chest, such as the adrenal gland and bones, is stage 4B. Any tumor size and any node status still give stage 4B once the cancer has reached several sites beyond the chest. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 25, 28, 29"},{"q":"Which World Health Organization pulmonary hypertension group includes connective tissue disease, human immunodeficiency virus infection and schistosomiasis?","choices":["Group 1","Group 2","Group 3","Group 5"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Group 1, pulmonary arterial hypertension, is pre-capillary disease with an obliterative and proliferative mechanism. Its causes are idiopathic disease, connective tissue disease, human immunodeficiency virus infection and schistosomiasis. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 19"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman with a confirmed large pulmonary embolism has an electrocardiogram showing a deep S wave in lead I and a Q wave with an inverted T wave in lead III (S1Q3T3). What does this pattern indicate?","choices":["A sensitive screen for embolism","Left ventricular hypertrophy","An inferior myocardial infarction","Severe right ventricular strain"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. S1Q3T3 suggests severe right ventricular strain rather than embolism as such: it is highly specific but has low sensitivity. In a patient with a large embolism, it reflects the acutely loaded right ventricle. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"Which measure belongs to the supportive care of an adult with coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":["Higher hemoglobin targets","Lavage to remove the coal dust","Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care for coal workers' pneumoconiosis includes oxygen, pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, because no effective curative treatment is available. Lung transplant may be indicated in severe cases. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 56, 74"},{"q":"A 71-year-old man with a 60 pack-year history has 2 months of cough, wheezing and blood-streaked sputum. Computed tomography shows a central mass arising in the right main bronchus, and sputum cytology shows malignant cells. Which lung cancer type is most likely?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Squamous cell carcinoma sits centrally and often originates in a bronchus, has the strongest association with smoking, and causes cough, wheezing and hemoptysis once it obstructs the bronchus. Unlike the other lung cancers it can be caught with sputum testing, so malignant cells in his sputum from a central bronchial mass point to it. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 30, 33, 39, 40"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman takes hydrocortisone for a chronic inflammatory condition and must stay on a corticosteroid. She has a metabolic alkalosis. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.49 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 47 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 34 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which change to her corticosteroid is most appropriate?","choices":["Keep hydrocortisone at the same dose","Raise the hydrocortisone dose","Switch to prednisone","Switch to cortisone"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. If a patient with metabolic alkalosis requires corticosteroids, lower the dose or change to one with less mineralocorticoid activity: prednisone or methylprednisolone rather than cortisone or hydrocortisone. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 53, 54, 55"},{"q":"A 61-year-old woman with a body mass index of 43 kg/m2, hypersomnolence and a raised serum bicarbonate is being evaluated for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Which test is the gold standard for her?","choices":["Sleep study with oxygen saturation alone","Daytime arterial blood gas alone","Home sleep apnea test","Sleep study with carbon dioxide monitoring"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Polysomnography with continuous nocturnal carbon dioxide monitoring is the gold standard for obesity hypoventilation syndrome. Following carbon dioxide through the night captures the hypoventilation of the syndrome alongside the obstructive events that coexist in about 90% of patients. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 32, 31, 30, 8"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman with a newly diagnosed lung cancer is meeting a radiation oncologist. Her son has read that some lung cancers respond far better to radiotherapy than others, and he asks which type benefits most. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Small cell carcinoma","Squamous cell carcinoma","Large cell carcinoma","Adenocarcinoma"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Small cell carcinoma responds better to chemotherapy and radiation than the three non-small cell cancers, and radiation works best when it is in limited stage. Non-small cell cancers do not respond well to chemotherapy or radiation, which serve as adjuncts to surgery or for stage 4. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 33, 35, 41"},{"q":"An 83-year-old woman who still smokes has a 60 pack-year history. She has no symptoms and no known lung nodule. Her daughter asks whether she should start yearly lung cancer screening scans. Which describes her eligibility?","choices":["Not eligible because of age","Eligible every 5 years","Not eligible while she smokes","Eligible as a current smoker"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Screening is recommended for adults 55 to 80 with at least a 30 pack-year history who smoke now or quit within the last 15 years. Her 60 pack-years and current smoking would qualify, but at 83 she is beyond the upper age limit of 80. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slide 18"},{"q":"A first primary spontaneous pneumothorax has an apex-to-cupola distance greater than 3 cm with significant dyspnea and chest pain. Which treatment is listed?","choices":["Observation with oxygen","Chest tube insertion","Open thoracotomy","Thoracoscopic pleurodesis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A moderate or large primary spontaneous pneumothorax, more than 3 cm at the apex, with moderate or severe symptoms such as significant chest pain and dyspnea, is treated with chest tube insertion. A recurrent primary pneumothorax instead goes to thoracoscopic surgery with pleurodesis. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 50, 53, 55"},{"q":"A 34-year-old woman with systemic lupus erythematosus has 2 days of sharp, well-localized pain over the left chest wall that worsens with deep breathing, coughing and sneezing. She is tachypneic, with limited chest wall expansion on the left. A pleural friction rub (a grating, creaking sound) is heard in both phases of respiration and disappears when she holds her breath. Her chest radiograph is normal. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Costochondritis","Pericarditis","Pleurisy","Pneumothorax"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pleurisy is inflammation of the parietal pleura, and inflammatory disorders such as systemic lupus erythematosus are among its causes. Well-localized sharp pain made worse by breathing, coughing and sneezing, limited expansion on the affected side and a pleural friction rub fit, and a rub that disappears with breath holding comes from the pleura. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 23, 26"},{"q":"Which imaging finding is characteristic of silicosis?","choices":["Pleural plaques at the bases","Patchy pleural-based honeycombing","Normal heart with air bronchograms","Eggshell calcification of hilar nodes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Silicosis imaging shows nodular opacities throughout, silicotic nodules of 1 to 10 mm forming nodular pulmonary fibrosis, and eggshell calcification of the periphery of the hilar lymph nodes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"A 28-year-old woman lies relaxed with her eyes closed at lights-out and then sleeps on into her deepest non-rapid eye movement stage. Which sequence of dominant waves is her electroencephalogram expected to show?","choices":["Delta, then theta, then alpha","Beta, then alpha, then theta","Alpha, then theta, then delta","Alpha, then beta, then delta"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Relaxed wakefulness with the eyes closed shows alpha waves; stages N1 and N2 show theta waves; and stage N3, the deepest non-rapid eye movement stage, shows delta waves. The frequency of the tracing becomes progressively slower as she passes from rest into deep sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 28, 27, 29"},{"q":"After many hours of activity, activating-system neurons fatigue and their feedback loop with the cortex fades. What happens next?","choices":["Rapid eye movement sleep starts at once","The activating nuclei are released","Sleep-promoting centers take over","Unarousable coma sets in"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. With the activating neurons fatigued and the positive feedback between the mesencephalic reticular nuclei and the cerebral cortex fading, the sleep-promoting centers take over and the brain makes the transition from wakefulness to sleep. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 5, 7, 9, 19"},{"q":"A 33-year-old woman's partner notices that late in the night her eyes dart rapidly under closed lids, her breathing becomes irregular and her fingers twitch. When woken, she describes a vivid dream. Which stage was she in?","choices":["Non-rapid eye movement stage N3","Rapid eye movement sleep","Quiet, relaxed wakefulness","Non-rapid eye movement stage N2"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Rapid eye movement sleep is the active form of sleep associated with vivid dreaming, rapid eye movements, irregular muscle movements, and irregular heart rate and breathing. Late-night timing fits too, since rapid eye movement periods lengthen in the later cycles. — CMS 1 Phases of Sleep - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 9, 10, 13, 14, 7, 28"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with a 50 pack-year history has a central lung mass, and biopsy shows small cell carcinoma. Over 2 months he has developed progressive muscle weakness, worst in his legs. Testing confirms Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome (autoimmune muscle weakness, particularly in the legs). He asks what causes the weakness. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Antibodies destroy muscle fibers","Low sodium weakens the muscles","Nerve calcium channels are attacked","Tumor compresses the spinal cord"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Lambert-Eaton myasthenic syndrome is a rare autoimmune disorder in which the immune system attacks calcium channels on nerve endings at the neuromuscular junction. The non-functioning channels prevent release of acetylcholine, so the muscles cannot contract properly, producing progressive weakness, particularly in the legs. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 13, 41, 46, 47, 48"},{"q":"A 64-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and chronic hypoxemia has a hematocrit of 58% (normal 41-50%). Echocardiography shows right ventricular hypertrophy. How does his blood count add to the load on his right ventricle?","choices":["It raises blood viscosity","It lowers blood viscosity","It dilates pulmonary arteries","It lowers oxygen content"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Chronic hypoxia drives polycythemia, and the extra red cells raise blood viscosity and the workload of the heart. That compounds the structural and hypoxic rise in pulmonary resistance his right ventricle already pumps against. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with rheumatoid arthritis has taken methotrexate for 6 years. She now has progressive exertional dyspnea, a dry cough, bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. She asks whether her arthritis medicine could be involved. Which response is accurate?","choices":["Yes, methotrexate can cause this disease","No, methotrexate protects the lungs","Yes, methotrexate deposits dust in the lungs","No, drug injury needs cancer chemotherapy"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is used for many autoimmune diseases, including lupus and rheumatoid arthritis, and it has also been implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease. Rheumatoid arthritis is itself a connective tissue cause, so her disease and her treatment both belong in the differential. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 18, 50, 60"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has received large volumes of normal saline for 3 days after abdominal surgery. She also takes acetaminophen for pain, has nasogastric suction, and takes a loop diuretic for edema. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.32 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 30 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Serum sodium 140 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 115 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L), bicarbonate 15 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of her care should be limited?","choices":["Her nasogastric suction","Further large-volume normal saline","Her acetaminophen for pain","Her loop diuretic for edema"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Low pH and bicarbonate make a metabolic acidosis, and the gap is 140 - (15 + 115) = 10, normal, with a high chloride. Saline infusion is a normal-gap cause, so the infusion feeding the acidosis is the part to limit. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 45, 46, 53"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has abdominal bloating, early satiety, profound fatigue and an episode of syncope while climbing stairs. His lungs are clear on auscultation. His chest film shows enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning, and right heart catheterization shows an elevated mean pulmonary artery pressure with a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg. What is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Acute pulmonary embolism","Left heart failure","Cardiogenic pulmonary edema","Right heart failure"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Bloating, early satiety, profound fatigue and exertional syncope with clear lungs are right heart failure. Enlarged central pulmonary arteries with peripheral pruning, and a normal wedge pressure with a high mean pulmonary artery pressure, confirm a pre-capillary, right-sided process. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 23, 35, 50"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman in the intensive care unit with septic shock is febrile, hypotensive and breathing 32 times a minute. Arterial blood gas: pH 7.40 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 20 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 12 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L); sodium 138 mEq/L (normal 135 to 145 mEq/L), chloride 104 mEq/L (normal 98 to 106 mEq/L). Which acid-base disorders are present?","choices":["Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory alkalosis","Respiratory alkalosis with metabolic compensation","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with appropriate compensation","Raised-gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory acidosis"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Gap = 138 - (104 + 12) = 22, raised, so a metabolic acidosis is present despite the normal pH. Expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x 12 + 8 = 26, range 24 to 28; her 20 is below it, so a concurrent respiratory alkalosis is present. Sepsis is the classic setting, and the two pull the pH back to normal. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 26, 28, 49"},{"q":"A 50-year-old woman has progressive exertional dyspnea. Echocardiography estimates a high pulmonary artery systolic pressure with an enlarged right ventricle, and her chest film, pulmonary function tests and ventilation-perfusion scan are normal. Which test is required to confirm pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension?","choices":["Right heart catheterization","B-type natriuretic peptide level","Computed tomographic angiography","Repeat echocardiography"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Right heart catheterization is the mandatory confirmation: it measures the mean pulmonary artery pressure, the pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and pulmonary vascular resistance. A high mean pressure with a normal wedge pressure confirms pre-capillary pulmonary hypertension. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 24, 36"},{"q":"A 76-year-old man has had interstitial lung disease for 9 years, and his dyspnea now occurs at rest. An echocardiogram shows right heart failure. What does the right heart failure indicate about his lung disease?","choices":["It is at an early stage","It has entered an acute phase","It is at an advanced stage","It is now cardiogenic edema"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Right heart failure appears with advanced interstitial lung disease, and dyspnea that has spread from exertion to rest also marks progression. Early in the disease the examination may be normal, so these findings place him late in a long, chronic course. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 23, 24, 73"},{"q":"Which pulmonary embolism risk category is normotensive but shows right ventricular dysfunction or elevated troponin?","choices":["Transient, provoked risk","Submassive (intermediate risk)","Massive (high risk)","Low risk"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Submassive, or intermediate-risk, embolism is normotensive (systolic blood pressure above 90) but shows right ventricular dysfunction on echocardiography or computed tomographic pulmonary angiography, or myocardial necrosis (elevated troponin or B-type natriuretic peptide). It carries a high risk of sudden deterioration. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slides 58, 59"},{"q":"A 55-year-old farmer with farmer's lung has stopped handling moldy hay, but he remains hypoxic with cyanosis, tachypnea and tachycardia, and his disease is judged severe. Which treatment may be helpful now?","choices":["Return to work with a mask","Corticosteroids","Early prone positioning","High-frequency oscillation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. After the agent is identified and avoided, corticosteroids may be helpful in severe cases of hypersensitivity pneumonitis, with other immunosuppressants and supportive care such as oxygen. His persistent hypoxia, cyanosis, tachypnea and tachycardia despite avoidance mark severe disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 65, 68, 69, 71, 74"},{"q":"A 43-year-old woman with habitual snoring and daytime sleepiness has a home sleep apnea test that does not show obstructive sleep apnea. Her symptoms persist. What is the best next step?","choices":["Polysomnogram","Modafinil","Repeat home test","Reassurance"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. At-home kits will not always diagnose mild obstructive sleep apnea; they are most helpful in moderate to severe disease. A negative home study in a woman with ongoing snoring and sleepiness therefore needs a polysomnogram, which can also find other sleep disorders a home kit cannot assess. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slides 8, 11"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has a chylothorax after thoracic surgery, managed with a chest tube. Over several days the tube has drained large volumes of milky fluid, and she is losing weight. Which practice should be avoided?","choices":["Intermittent thoracentesis","Low long-chain fat diet","Excessive drainage","Octreotide therapy"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Chylothorax is managed with chest tube placement or intermittent thoracentesis while avoiding excessive drainage, because excessive loss of chyle can impact nutritional status. Her large volumes and weight loss show that risk. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 105, 106"},{"q":"Which pair of findings fits a tension pneumothorax?","choices":["Hypotension and neck emphysema","Hypotension and tracheal deviation","Bradycardia and flat neck veins","Hypertension and tracheal deviation"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In a tension pneumothorax the mediastinum shifts to the contralateral side, venous return and cardiac output fall, and the patient shows hypotension, tracheal deviation and neck vein distention, with decreased or absent breath sounds. Those bedside signs are enough to act on. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 64, 77"},{"q":"A 38-year-old man with generalized anxiety disorder and interstitial pulmonary fibrosis is brought in after a car crash with a flail chest (a rib segment moving inward on inspiration). Arterial blood gas: pH 7.31 (normal 7.35 to 7.45), partial pressure of carbon dioxide 56 mm Hg (normal 35 to 45 mm Hg), bicarbonate 27 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). Which part of his history explains this blood gas?","choices":["The flail chest","Pain from his injuries","His interstitial fibrosis","His anxiety disorder"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Low pH with a high carbon dioxide is a respiratory acidosis, and the bicarbonate of 27 fits an acute rise. Respiratory acidosis comes from decreased ventilation, and thoracic cage injury such as a flail chest is a listed cause. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slides 31, 32, 39, 40"},{"q":"In acidosis, how does hemoglobin's affinity for oxygen change, and what happens to tissue oxygen delivery?","choices":["Affinity rises; delivery falls","Affinity falls; delivery rises","Affinity rises; delivery rises","Affinity falls; delivery falls"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. In acidosis hemoglobin has a decreased affinity for oxygen, so it gives oxygen up more readily and oxygen delivery to the tissues increases. Alkalosis does the reverse: higher affinity and less delivery to the tissues. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 5"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman who quit smoking 30 years ago has a 2.4-cm spiculated (spiky) nodule in the periphery of the left upper lobe that involves the pleura. Biopsy shows a non-small cell carcinoma. Which stain result would support adenocarcinoma?","choices":["Negative mucicarmine stain","Mucin on mucicarmine stain","Large anaplastic cells","Small cells without nucleoli"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Histology may include a mucicarmine stain: mucin production is related to adenocarcinoma, while negative staining may point to large cell carcinoma. A suspicious solid spiculated nodule in the periphery that involves the pleura is the picture of an invasive adenocarcinoma, which grows from type II pneumocytes. — Pulmonary Nodules and Neoplasms 2026.pptx, Slides 32, 33, 34, 42"},{"q":"A 34-year-old man has bilateral hilar adenopathy on chest film and sarcoidosis is suspected, but his clinical and radiographic findings also raise the possibility of lymphoma. What is the next step?","choices":["Repeat chest film in a year","Oral corticosteroids","Tissue biopsy","Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme level"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Biopsy is essential whenever clinical and radiographic findings suggest an alternative diagnosis such as lymphoma. Transbronchial lung or fine-needle node biopsy also confirms sarcoidosis itself by showing noncaseating granulomas, so tissue settles the question either way. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 45, 47, 48"},{"q":"A 64-year-old woman's polysomnogram shows events with no airflow. In each, there is no respiratory effort during the first part of the event, and effort resumes during the latter part while airflow is still absent. How are these events classified?","choices":["Hypopnea","Mixed apnea","Obstructive apnea","Central apnea"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A mixed apnea has no airflow, with a lack of respiratory effort during the first part of the event and resumption of effort during the latter part. Her events begin like central apneas and end like obstructive ones, which is exactly the mixed pattern. — CMS I Obstructive Sleep Apnea & Obesity Hypoventilation Syndrome - Shah Fallsv.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"A 66-year-old woman has taken nitrofurantoin for years to prevent recurrent urinary tract infections. Over several months she has developed progressive exertional dyspnea and a dry cough, with bibasilar inspiratory crackles and a restrictive pattern on pulmonary function testing. She has no occupational, bird or mold exposures. What is the most likely cause of her interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Sarcoidosis","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis","Drug-induced lung disease","Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Nitrofurantoin is one of the treatment-related causes of interstitial lung disease, beside radiation, methotrexate, amiodarone and chemotherapeutics, and it is also named among drugs that cause pulmonary toxicity. Years of use followed by exertional dyspnea, dry cough, crackles and restriction fit a drug cause. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 12, 23, 24, 35, 40, 72"},{"q":"A 30-year-old man has a primary spontaneous pneumothorax. His respiratory rate is 18 breaths per minute, heart rate 128 beats per minute, blood pressure 116/72 mm Hg and oxygen saturation 97% on room air, and he speaks in whole sentences. How is he classified by the American College of Chest Physicians stability definition?","choices":["Unstable: oxygen saturation","Unstable: respiratory rate","Stable","Unstable: heart rate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The heart-rate criterion has two bounds: over 60 and under 120 beats per minute. His rate of 128 is above 120, so he fails it even though his respiratory rate of 18, normal pressure, saturation of 97% and full sentences pass; anyone who misses a criterion is unstable. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slide 51"},{"q":"A 77-year-old man has a new right pleural effusion with no clear clinical cause, and diagnostic thoracentesis is performed. Pleural fluid protein is 1.8 g/dL with serum protein 6.9 g/dL (normal 6.0-8.3 g/dL). Pleural fluid lactate dehydrogenase is 80 units/L with serum lactate dehydrogenase 190 units/L (normal 140-280 units/L). How is the effusion classified by Light's criteria?","choices":["Transudate, no criterion met","Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase ratio","Exudate, by lactate dehydrogenase level","Exudate, by protein ratio"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Protein ratio 1.8 / 6.9 = 0.26, not above 0.5. Lactate dehydrogenase ratio 80 / 190 = 0.42, not above 0.6. Fluid lactate dehydrogenase 80 is under two-thirds of the 280 upper limit, about 187. With all of Light's criteria absent, the fluid is a low-protein transudate. — Pleural Disorders 2026.pptx, Slides 96, 101, 110, 112"},{"q":"A 69-year-old man with chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and cor pulmonale uses continuous home oxygen. He has had three exacerbations this year, each set off by a respiratory infection. Besides the oxygen, which plan addresses the root of his cor pulmonale?","choices":["Strict fluid restriction alone","Phosphodiesterase-5 inhibitor therapy","Thrombolysis and anticoagulation","Bronchodilators and prompt infection care"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The prime directive in cor pulmonale is to treat the underlying lung disease, because the right ventricle cannot recover while the lungs keep loading it. For chronic obstructive pulmonary disease that means bronchodilators and aggressive treatment of respiratory infections, the triggers of his exacerbations, alongside continuous oxygen. — Pulmonary Vascular Disorders 2026 - Gray.pptx, Slide 15"},{"q":"A 71-year-old woman with exertional dyspnea, clubbing and bibasilar inspiratory crackles is diagnosed with idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis. Which chest film finding fits her disease?","choices":["Interstitial markings in the lower lung fields","Bilateral hilar adenopathy alone","Eggshell calcification of the hilar nodes","Nodular opacities in the upper lung fields"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. In idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis the radiograph shows increased interstitial markings in the lower lung fields, with progressive fibrosis over several areas, matching her bibasilar crackles. Upper lung field nodules belong to coal and silica dust instead. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 27, 34, 45, 53, 63"},{"q":"A metabolic acidosis has a bicarbonate of 14 mEq/L (normal 22 to 26 mEq/L). What is the expected carbon dioxide range?","choices":["19 to 23 mm Hg","27 to 31 mm Hg","31 to 35 mm Hg","38 to 42 mm Hg"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Winter formula: expected carbon dioxide = 1.5 x bicarbonate + 8, plus or minus 2. Here 1.5 x 14 = 21, plus 8 = 29, so the expected range is 27 to 31 mm Hg. A measured value inside it means metabolic acidosis only. — PAJ5510 Acid Base Disorders II MBF2026sc.pptx, Slide 49"}]};
